ID: B2-Ch09a-Intro
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch8_energizer == 3) {
 

"Barry?" Kate lowers her hood as I walk up. "What are you doing? Weren't you asleep?"

"Couldn't sleep," I say. "Came out for some air."

"Why not?"

"Visitor in the middle of the night. Tent got crowded."

"A visitor?" Kate's head turns toward the tree line, then back to me. "I've been on watch the entire shift. Nobody walked past this perimeter."

"Remember how Eiden hides his presence?" I say. "Melindra uses the same trick."

"Melindra." Her voice drops an octave. "She came into your tent and threw you out?"

"No, hold on," I say. "Nobody threw me out. I walked out on my own."

"Why on earth would you do that? Tell her to leave and go back to sleep."

}
#if(v_b2_ch8_energizer == 1) {
 

"Barry." Kate stands by the cold fire pit, eyes locked on my flap. "What is Melindra doing in your tent?"

Everyone is up. Daren is rolling his bedroll; Hadrik is chewing dried meat; Leila has her arms crossed, watching the faint flutter around my shoulders. If my aura twitches, she catches it.

"She forgot her tent," I say, keeping my chin level and avoiding Leila's gaze. "She needed somewhere to sleep."

"And you offered yours." Kate's voice is flat ice. "Just like that."

"She didn't have anywhere else to go." Sweat prickles along my hairline. "She's right there, isn't she?"

Leila watches me, expressionless. No glowing ink in the air, no tell in her shoulders. Whether she sees right through the lie or is waiting for me to dig a deeper hole, I cannot tell.

Two arms wrap around my bicep from behind. Melindra presses into my side, resting her chin on my shoulder.

"Darling," she says with a tragic pout. "Why hide what we shared? Did last night mean nothing to you?"

"Barry." Kate's knuckles go white at her sides. "What is she talking about?"

"Sleep," I say through gritted teeth, digging my fingers into Melindra's forearm. "She is talking about sleeping."

"Why so cold, my love?" Melindra bats her eyelashes. "Have you already forgotten who kept me awake until dawn?"

"Kept you awake until--" Hadrik chokes on his jerky. A wheeze escapes his throat, then bursts into a loud, gut-shaking roar of laughter.

"Until dawn! Ahahahahaha!" Arraka's screech erupts from Flower's chest. "Oh, the sheer stamina! Ahahaha!"

"I'm leaving," Kate snaps. She turns on her heel and marches into the woods, while Hadrik slaps his thighs and Arraka cackles from the amulet.

}
#if(v_b2_ch8_energizer == 2) {
 

"Barry." Kate's glare cuts through the morning fog. "What is Melindra doing in your tent?"

"Wait," I say, raising a hand. "I know how it looks, but--"

"Settle yourself, ice mage, nothing happened," Melindra says, crawling through the flap and stretching her arms above her head. "He gave me a patch of floor. That is the whole story."

"And why would he need to do that?" Kate asks.

"Ask Barry for the thrilling details," Melindra says, dusting off her silks. "I'll be by the river picking up stones. Have fun."

"Well?" Kate crosses her arms as Melindra strolls off. "What details?"

"Short version: she forgot to pack a tent," I say. "She didn't want to ask Daren or Hadrik because of yesterday's bickering, so she showed up at mine."

"And you let her stay. Just like that."

"She had nowhere else to sleep," I say. "What was I supposed to do, kick her into the dirt like a stray dog?"

Kate's eyes narrow, weighing the words.

Glowing blue letters trace through the air beside her. "He is telling the truth," Leila writes. "His aura is steady."

Kate exhales, the tension easing out of her shoulders. "If Leila says you're telling the truth, that settles it. I apologize for suspecting you."

"Don't worry about it," I say. "I'd suspect me too."

"Right, then!" Hadrik claps his broad hands together. "Show's over, pack it in! We've got ground to cover."

Flower skips over as the camp stirs. "Barry, are you hungry? I can make you breakfast!"

"I have rations, but I'd be an idiot to turn down magic cooking. What are we having?"

"The arena memories gave me ideas!" Flower beams. "Ever had trampler stew or archer porcupine pie? Real palace food. Give me two seconds!"

Flames flicker at her fingertips, and a steaming bowl and pastry settle into my hands. She darts off toward the river to feed Melindra. The stew is rich and peppered, but overhead the canopy sits beneath an iron sky. Heavy, bruise-colored clouds blot out every trace of morning sun. A summer storm is rolling in, and the air smells like damp ozone.

I finish eating and crawl back into the tent to pack my gear. When I reach for my pack, a second leather sack sits dumped squarely on top of it. Melindra's backpack. She must have floated it through the flap while I was dead to the world.

}
#if(v_b2_ch8_energizer == 0) {
 

The scrape of tent pegs and rustling canvas wakes me. When I unhook the flap, the woods are dim under a thick canopy of charcoal clouds. The sun rose an hour ago, but not a sliver of light cuts through the overcast. Most of the camp is already packed and fed.

I step outside, and Melindra darts over with an exaggerated grin, locking both hands around my arm and leaning in hard.

"Barry, darling, what a night!" she announces. "Did you sleep well after I left? I slept like a baby."

"Barry?" Kate steps between two trees, her gaze fixing on us like a blade. "What is Melindra talking about?"

"Oh, surely it's obvious," Melindra purrs. "You saw me leave his tent. Put two and two together."

"Let go of me," I say, prying her fingers off my sleeve. "Kate, she came in last night because she didn't have a tent, and I kicked her out. Nothing happened."

Kate's eyes narrow, searching my face.

Glowing blue script appears between us. "He is telling the truth," Leila writes. "His aura is clear."

"If Leila vouches for you, I believe you," Kate says. "I apologize for suspecting you."

"Hmph," Melindra pouts. "Boring."

She ducks into my tent and emerges a moment later with her backpack slung over one shoulder. She must have floated it inside while I was asleep. She takes two steps toward the river, then halts and spins back around.

"Almost forgot!" She strolls back with a sweet smile.

Her palm taps the top of my left shoulder. The moment she pulls away, a deafening gale roars straight into both my ears. The trees around us are barely swaying, but inside my skull it sounds like a hurricane funnel.

Melindra leans in close, her lips practically brushing my cheek.

"That's for the bugs last night," she shouts over the roar. "Enjoy your morning!"

"Hey!" I lunge after her, but she skips away. "Undo it! Get back here!"

She waves without looking back. Around me, Daren's mouth moves, but only rushing air reaches my head. He notices my blank stare, steps up, and yells at the top of his lungs.

"What did she do to you?"

"I don't know!" I shout back. "Some wind trick! It's roaring right in my ears, I can't hear a word!"

}
choice(""Because I didn't want to kick her out like a dog, when she didn't have a place to sleep."", B2-Ch09a-Dog, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Dog, v_checkpoint_rich = 0, v_b2_ch9_dog = 0) if (v_b2_ch8_energizer == 3)
