choice("Continue", B2-Ch09a-Kick, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Kick, v_checkpoint_rich = 0) if (v_b2_ch8_energizer == 0)


ID: B2-Ch09a-Dog
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch9_dog == 0) {
 "Because I didn't want to kick her out like a dog, when she didn't have a place to sleep."

"No place to sleep?" Kate asks. "Where's her tent?"

"Forgot to pack one," I say.

"That's what this is about?" Kate shakes her head. "Couldn't she ask Daren or Hadrik?"

"They aren't exactly on speaking terms after yesterday," I say.

"Unbelievable." Kate looks back toward the camp. "So you gave up your bed. What's the plan, stand night watch until dawn so the stillwater can sleep?"

"Not all night," I say. "When Hadrik and Daren take the next shift, I'll have them pitch a spare for her. But since I was already awake, I figured I'd come talk to you."

"Oh?" Her brow arches. "About what?"

"As you like. It feels like ages since we had a quiet conversation."

}
#if(v_b2_ch9_dog == 1) {
 "Because I didn't want to waste any more time arguing with her."

"So you gave up a full night's sleep to avoid an argument?" Kate asks.

"I'm not staying out all night," I say. "When Hadrik and Daren take over, I'll get them to set up a spare tent for her so I can get back to mine."

"A tent?" Kate blinks. "She forgot her tent?"

"Exactly," I say. "And since I was already up, I came over to talk."

"Oh?" Her brow arches. "About what?"

"As you like. It feels like ages since we had a quiet conversation."

}
#if(v_b2_ch9_dog == 2) {
 "Because I figured I may as well come chat with you, given that I was already up."

"Oh?" Her brow arches. "About what?"

"As you like. It feels like ages since we had a quiet conversation."

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
 "It has been," Kate says quietly. "I think the last time was after the collars came off in the sacred woods. Back when... Rose was with us."

The name lands like cold iron in my ribs. I haven't let myself dwell on Rose since that toast with Enrique in her garden. I promised myself I wouldn't run from the memory, but the mind finds its own exits.

"Yeah," I manage. "When Rose was here."

The woods go dead quiet between us.

"How are things with Leila?" I ask, pulling the topic away from the silence.

"Good," Kate says, and the ghost of a smile touches her mouth. "Really good. We've been catching up on everything since the Beacon. Years of stories."

"So you're friends again?"

"What do you mean, again?"

"You barely said two words to each other at first," I say. "I figured you left things on a bad note before she escaped."

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
 "It has been," Kate says. "I think the last time was after the collars came off in the sacred woods. Everything since has been a blur. Objectives, the arena, Tyrath's declaration..."

"And reuniting with Leila," I say. "How is that going?"

"Good," Kate says, and a rare, genuine smile softens her face. "Really good. We've been catching up on everything since the Beacon. Years of stories."

"So you're friends again?"

"What do you mean, again?"

"You barely looked at each other at first," I say. "I figured there was bad blood from before she left."

}
"No, nothing like that," Kate says. She stares at her boots. "We weren't close back then. I spent every hour with Diane and her brother. The overseers kept Leila isolated because she was lessathi. But when they told us she died... gods, it wrecked me. Why did I believe them? Why didn't I look for her?"

"You wouldn't have found her any faster than you found Diane," I say. "She's been hidden on Varathia with her adoptive father the whole time. Don't tear yourself apart over it. She survived."

"I owe her father everything," Kate says softly. "I want to meet him in Ollendor. Just to thank him."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch09a-Notebook, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Notebook)


ID: B2-Ch09a-Notebook
TEXT:

She looks out across the dark tree line. "What about you? Any progress with your notebook?"

"Hard to say. Plenty of theory, not enough practical data. If I could unlock the magic stats on the device, testing would be straightforward. On my own, that lock isn't budging."

"Could Leila's father help?"

"Maybe. I'm not betting on it. The goblin general said those stats require high-tier lessathi authorization codes. An outcast mechanic living in hiding probably doesn't carry clearance keys."

Silence settles again. Melindra's words from the tent drift back into my head. "She has feelings for you."
I shouldn't touch that. Bringing it up guarantees an awkward disaster. But letting it sit unanswered guarantees it will rattle around in my skull for the next three weeks.
A subtle, indirect approach. Work it into the conversation naturally, casual, no pressure.

"So, Kate," I say.

"Yes?"

"You don't happen to have feelings for me, do you?"

Tactless. Direct hit. Absolutely zero subtlety.

Kate stares at me like I just grew a third ear. "...Have you lost your mind?"

"Wait, don't throw an icicle!" I hold up both hands. "It wasn't my idea. Melindra brought it up."

Her jaw tightens. "Melindra. What did she say?"

"She claimed that look you gave her yesterday--when she played the clingy partner--meant you were jealous."

"Oh." Kate's gaze drifts to the dark grass. Her expression closes off, guarded, calculating. "I see."

She lifts her head, locking eyes with me again.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch09a-Lucky, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Lucky)


ID: B2-Ch09a-Lucky
TEXT:

#if(v_ch11_romance_rose == 1 && v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
 "I was shocked when I saw it, yes," Kate says. "You'd been talking to her privately minutes before, and I wondered if you'd forgotten the promise you made Rose in Thilias. You told Rose you'd come back for her when the tournament ends."

"I remember," I say. "I promised."

"Keep it," Kate says. "She teases, but she took you at your word. She'll be waiting."

"I know. I'm not breaking it."

"I know Melindra was playing a game yesterday," Kate continues, "but stay on your guard with her. She isn't predictable. And as for me... you don't need to worry about any feelings. I'm happy for you and Rose. Just make sure you survive long enough to keep your word."

"Don't you mean survive long enough to win?" I smirk.

"Sure." A faint smile tugs at the corner of her lips. "Win."

She weaves frost between her fingers, shaping an ice dagger, and tosses it lightly from palm to palm.

"You're lucky I'm not in the running," she says. "With no protection magic and that crossbow, you'd never touch me while I'm hovering overhead in an ice cage raining icicles on your head."

}
#if(v_ch11_romance_rose == 0 || v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
 "I was startled when I saw the two of you, yes," Kate says. "You'd been alone with her just before, and I worried you were about to do something reckless. I know it was a joke now, but you still need to watch yourself around Melindra. Her motives are opaque. As for her claims... I look out for you because you're a friend, Barry. I don't want you making mistakes that get you killed."

"Hold on. 'Main' reason?" I raise an eyebrow. "Are there secondary reasons on file?"

She glares at me. "You are infuriating."

"You chose the phrasing, not me."

"Regardless of how you twist it," Kate says, "our focus belongs on the tournament and the objectives."

"Naturally."

"If you want to cross-examine my phrasing, wait until we survive this island."

"Don't you mean after I win?"

A faint smirk breaks through her scowl. "Right. After you win."

She weaves frost between her fingers, shaping an ice dagger, and tosses it lightly from palm to palm.

"You're lucky I'm not in the running," she says. "With no protection magic and that crossbow, you'd never touch me while I'm hovering overhead in an ice cage raining icicles on your head."

}
choice(""I don't know. I wouldn't be so sure about that."", B2-Ch09a-Winner, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Winner, v_b2_ch9_defeat = 1)
choice(""Yeah, I probably couldn't last ten seconds against you in an actual fight."", B2-Ch09a-Winner, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Winner, v_b2_ch9_defeat = 0)


ID: B2-Ch09a-Winner
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch9_defeat == 1) {
 "I don't know. I wouldn't be so sure about that."

"No?"

"Depends on the terrain," I say. "Underground or inside a fortress, flight is off the table. In the open, starting distance decides it. You take a beat to raise barriers against sudden rushes. If I close the gap before your casting settles, I have an opening. Wait--this is purely hypothetical, right? You wouldn't actually try to kill me if the brackets paired us up?"

"No," Kate says. "Would you?"

"I couldn't. No."

"Then we're both fortunate," she says, letting the ice dagger dissolve into vapor.

}
#if(v_b2_ch9_defeat == 0) {
 "Yeah, I probably couldn't last ten seconds against you in an actual fight."

"Admitting defeat that easily?" Kate arches an eyebrow. "From a man planning to win the Magium?"

"We aren't fighting each other," I say. "No reason to pretend I wouldn't end up skewered. Though--speaking of raining icicles--that was hypothetical, right? You wouldn't actually try to kill me if the tournament pitted us together?"

"Of course not."

She watches me for a quiet second.

"Would you?" she asks.

"I couldn't. No."

"Then we're both fortunate," she says, letting the ice dagger dissolve into vapor.

}
"Daren won't be that simple," Kate adds quietly. "You two are the only ones here chasing the prize. If you both reach the end, only one person walks away with the Magium. You'll have to face each other."

"I know."

We sit in the quiet for a while, until a tent zipper rattles on the other side of camp. Daren steps out into the gray light, stretching his shoulders before walking over.

"Barry?" Daren halts a few paces back. "What are you doing up? Kate has first watch."

"Couldn't sleep," I say. "Your shift isn't for an hour and a half."

"I was running the arena fight through my head," Daren admits. "Mistakes we made, angles we missed. Couldn't rest, so I came to relieve Kate early."

"Well, my excuse involves an uninvited guest," I say.

I give him the short version: Melindra showing up without a tent, taking my floor, and leaving me to join Kate.

"Right," Daren sighs. "Barry, help me pitch a spare from my pack. Kate, go get some sleep."

"Gladly," Kate says, pushing off the log. "Good luck with... the tenant."

She slips into her tent while Daren and I haul a spare canvas from his pack and stake it down. Once it's secure, I head back to mine and nudge the bundle on the ground.

"Melindra. Up."

"Mmmph..." A sleepy groan muffles into the blanket. "Five more minutes..."

"You can sleep until noon in your own tent. Out."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch09a-Dismantling, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Dismantling)


ID: B2-Ch09a-Dismantling
TEXT:

"Don't have a tent..." Melindra mumbles. "Told you, forgot..."

"Daren's spare," I say. "I asked him. He set it up for you."

"He did?" One eye opens, sharp and surprised.

"Go on. He's waiting outside."

"Fine, fine." She rubs her face, pulling herself up. "I'm moving."

The moment she steps through the flap, I collapse back onto my bedroll and sleep like stone.

Morning comes without sun. Gray gloom filters through the canvas. When I unhook the flap, heavy charcoal clouds hang low over the canopy, thick enough to choke out all morning light. The camp is already packing up.

I step into the clearing, and Melindra marches straight toward me. The mocking smirk is gone; her face is uncharacteristically serious.

"Listen," she says, halting a pace away. "I didn't intend to kick you out of your own tent."

"I know," I say. "It's fine."

"It isn't fine. I wanted a quiet corner to sleep for a few hours and slip out before anyone noticed. I didn't plan on waking half the camp."

"You got a tent in the end," I say. "What does it matter?"

"You helped me out," she says, her jaw set. "I didn't want to make things difficult for you."

I study her face. "So you actually can give a damn about someone else."

She blinks. "What?"

"Why act like this if you do?"

"Act like what?"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch09a-Favor, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Favor)


ID: B2-Ch09a-Favor
TEXT:

"Since yesterday, you've gone out of your way to poke every hornets' nest in camp," I say. "You skip names, say whatever crosses your mind, and treat everyone's irritation like free entertainment."

"Why should I care if they're irritated?" Melindra crosses her arms. "I don't need them as friends. I need to reach Ollendor."

"Do you need them as enemies?"

"Eiden never held it against me."

"Most people aren't Eiden."

"That doesn't mean I have to rewrite myself to suit strangers," she snaps.

"Nobody's asking for a new personality," I say. "Just stop actively provoking the people walking beside you. Unless you'd rather spend every night on this road unable to ask a companion for a spare canvas?"

Her brow furrows, mouth tightening into a stubborn pout.

"Fine," she mutters. "I'll dial it back. A little. Only a little."

"Progress is progress," I say. "We should pack. The others look ready to move."

"Already ate," Melindra says, turning back toward my tent. "Get Flower to conjure you breakfast. She has royal recipes from the arena--trampler stew, porcupine pie. I'm getting my pack."

"When did you put your pack in my tent?"

"Floated it inside while you were snoring," she calls over her shoulder, ducking inside and coming back out with the leather bag. "Found it."

She heads toward the gravel bank to gather river stones. I take down my canvas, return it to Hadrik, and let Flower conjure a steaming bowl of stew. Once everyone has strapped their gear down, Melindra takes the lead along the river.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch09a-Pinecones, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Pinecones)


ID: B2-Ch09a-Energizer4
TEXT:

"Right," Daren says, rubbing his forehead. "I won't pry, but pack your gear and eat within the hour. We're moving out. And Melindra--next time, ask Hadrik or me for a spare tent instead of raiding people at midnight. Just a thought."

"I'll consider it!" Melindra beams.

"Come on, Hadrik, Flower," Daren sighs, heading toward his bedroll.

"Until dawn..." Hadrik wipes a tear of laughter from his beard, still chuckling as he turns away. "Gods, what a morning. Thanks for that, Barry. Truly."

"Any time," I say, flat.

Leila and Flower follow them, though Flower casts one troubled glance over her shoulder at me before she leaves.

"Well, that was lively!" Melindra stretches. "What's for breakfast? I'm starving."

"Are you serious?" I stare at her. "You're just going to brush that off?"

"Brush what off?" She looks at me with genuine, wide-eyed innocence.

"You dropped all of that in front of everyone on purpose! What was the point?"

"Their faces were hilarious," she says simply. "Especially the ice mage. Weren't you amused?"

Her eyes are completely clear. She isn't mocking me now--she genuinely cannot see why anyone would object.

choice(""All right, maybe it was a little funny, but that's beside the point!"", B2-Ch09a-Amusing, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Amusing, v_b2_ch9_amused = 1)
choice(""No, I did not find their reactions to be amusing at all!"", B2-Ch09a-Amusing, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Amusing, v_b2_ch9_amused = 0)


ID: B2-Ch09a-Amusing
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch9_amused == 1) {
 "All right, maybe it was a little funny, but that's beside the point!"

"How is it beside the point?" Melindra asks. "You have to enjoy the moment, Barry. Little sparks make life bearable. So your friends got ruffled. They'll survive. Well, most of them. The ice mage will probably hold a grudge, but I wasn't the one who slept with her enemy after finding out she fancied you."

"Kate does NOT have feelings for me!"

"Whatever helps you sleep, darling. Now, do you have food or do I dig through my pack? Jerky and dry cheese get old fast. Oh--the little girl with the banshee! Flower. She said she could conjure feasts like Eiden and the fox. I'll go grab her. She's the only one here who doesn't look ready to murder me, so she won't mind."

"Hold on," I say, catching her sleeve. "We aren't finished."

"Finished with what?"

"With this. Don't you see what happens when you treat people like punchlines? You make enemies out of everyone you meet. My friends will get over it, but what about you? Why push every button within reach? Can't you just act normal so people don't despise you on sight?"

"I don't need people to like me," she says.

"Do you need them to hate you?"

"Eiden never hated me for it."

"Most people aren't Eiden."

"Doesn't mean I'll turn into someone else just to keep strangers happy."

"Nobody asked for a saint," I say. "Just stop poking people for sport. Is that really impossible?"

Melindra tilts her head, considering me.

"No," she says slowly. "You admitted earlier that you found it funny. You get it, at least a little. What you need is to loosen up, Barry. Don't worry, I can help. You'll thank me later."

"I said a little funny!" I call after her as she slips away. "Hey! Where are you going?"

"Getting Flower," she waves. "Breakfast first, lectures later."

}
#if(v_b2_ch9_amused == 0) {
 "No, I did not find their reactions to be amusing at all!"

"You didn't?" She tilts her head, genuinely puzzled. "Why not?"

"Because they're my friends," I say.

"And? Friends poke each other. It's harmless."

"You didn't poke them, you threw a firecracker into a dry tent."

"Same difference," she says with a shrug. "They got ruffled. They'll survive. Well, most of them. The ice mage will probably hold a grudge, but I wasn't the one who slept with her enemy after finding out she fancied you."

"Kate does NOT have feelings for me!"

"Whatever helps you sleep, darling. Now, do you have food or do I dig through my pack? Jerky and dry cheese get old fast. Oh--the little girl with the banshee! Flower. She said she could conjure feasts like Eiden and the fox. I'll go grab her. She's the only one here who doesn't look ready to murder me, so she won't mind."

"Hold on," I say, catching her sleeve. "We aren't finished."

"Finished with what?"

"With this. Don't you see what happens when you treat people like punchlines? You make enemies out of everyone you meet. My friends will get over it, but what about you? Why push every button within reach? Can't you just act normal so people don't despise you on sight?"

"I don't need people to like me," she says.

"Do you need them to hate you?"

"Eiden never hated me for it."

"Most people aren't Eiden."

"Doesn't mean I'll turn into someone else just to keep strangers happy."

"Nobody asked for a saint," I say. "Just stop poking people for sport. Is that really impossible?"

Melindra studies me for a quiet moment, her mouth twitching into a pout.

"You really didn't find any of it funny?"

"Not in the slightest."

"Not even Hadrik choking on his meat?"

"No."

"Fine," she sighs. "I'll tone it down. A little. Only a little."

"That's something."

"I'm getting Flower," she says, turning away. "All this arguing gave me an appetite. Let's see if those royal recipes live up to the talk."

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch09a-Panicked, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Panicked)


ID: B2-Ch09a-Panicked
TEXT:

Melindra waves toward the river, where Flower is juggling sparks over the water. Flower trots over, looking up at me with wide, concerned green eyes.

"Barry, are you alright?" Flower asks softly. "Don't worry, the others will cool down. Hadrik was already laughing."

"It would have helped if someone kept her mouth shut," I say, glaring at Melindra. "Too late now."

"Be fair," Melindra says. "You gave yourself away before I opened my mouth. You're a terrible liar. I only added flair."

"I was perfectly calm!"

"No," Illuna's voice cuts in, colder, as the eyes shift to bright blue. "The lie was obvious without reading your aura. You cannot bluff when pressed."

"Melindra!" Arraka's voice rattles from the golden amulet. "Tell me--when you said all night, did you use an energizer on him?"

"What if I did?" Melindra asks.

"Hah! I knew it! Oh, this day is glorious."

Flower gasps, taking back control of the body. Her green eyes go wide with panic. "Barry, you took an energizer? Have you ever had one before?"

"No. Why?"

"First-time energizers have severe backlash!" Flower says, waving her hands. "Didn't you know?"

"Why would I study energizer backlash? I couldn't cast spells."

"Oh," Melindra taps her chin. "Right. Splitting headaches and violent nausea. It's been five centuries, I forgot."

"You forgot?" I stare at her. "What about everyone else you've cast it on?"

"I usually left before morning," Melindra says airily. "Don't worry. When the headache hits, I'll just cast it again."

"Cast it again!" Arraka shrieks with delight. "Ahahahahaha! Stop, my sides! Ahahaha!"

"You cannot let her cast a second one!" Flower's voice rises in terror. "Two in a row without sleep will wreck your system when the crash comes!"

"Your choice," Melindra says with a shrug. "Suffer on the march while watching for monsters, or crash tonight when we're safe behind camp wards."

choice("Ask her to cast a second energizing spell on me, after the side-effects kick in.", B2-Ch09a-Cast1, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Cast1, v_b2_ch9_cast1 = 1)
choice("Ask her to not cast a second energizing spell on me.", B2-Ch09a-Cast1, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Cast1, v_b2_ch9_cast1 = 0)


ID: B2-Ch09a-Cast1
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch9_cast1 == 1) {
 "I guess you're right," I say. "I'd rather crash tonight than drag everybody down if we get ambushed."

"Splendid!" Melindra says. "Now, what's for breakfast?"

"Arena recipes!" Flower beams. "Trampler stew and archer porcupine pie. Rare royal dishes! I'll have them ready in a second!"

We eat as the morning gloom thickens overhead. Dark, heavy clouds press against the canopy, swallowing every trace of sunlight. A summer storm is building; the air feels dense with damp ozone.

Once the plates are cleared, Melindra and I tackle my tent while Flower checks on the others.

"Let me grab my pack first," Melindra says, slipping inside.

"When did you float that in?"

"While you were out cold," she says, hauling the leather bag out. "Ready. Let's fold the canvas."

}
#if(v_b2_ch9_cast1 == 0) {
 "I'll tough it out," I say. "Better to handle the headache than make the crash worse later."

"Suit yourself," Melindra shrugs. "So what are we having?"

"Arena recipes!" Flower beams. "Trampler stew and archer porcupine pie. Rare royal dishes! I'll have them ready in a second!"

We eat as the morning gloom thickens overhead. Dark, heavy clouds press against the canopy, swallowing every trace of sunlight. A summer storm is building; the air feels dense with damp ozone.

Once the plates are cleared, Melindra and I tackle my tent while Flower checks on the others.

"Let me grab my pack first," Melindra says, slipping inside.

"When did you float that in?"

"While you were out cold," she says, hauling the leather bag out. "Ready. Let's fold the canvas."

}
We pack the tent and hand the pegs back to Hadrik. Daren is strapping on his cuirass, brow furrowed as he checks the gray sky. Kate sits near the trees, still cold and distant. Melindra takes the lead, and we move out along the riverbank.

A few minutes down the trail, Hadrik drops back and falls into step beside me.

"Listen, Barry," he says quietly, glancing ahead at the group. "I know I laughed back there, but I wasn't mocking you. Truth is, I'm impressed. You knew half the group distrusts her, and you went and did it anyway. For an entire night! Takes real stones to pull a stunt like that and not care who finds out. I can respect it."

"Uh... thanks?"

"Glad we cleared the air!" Hadrik thumps me hard on the shoulder blades, nearly knocking the wind out of me. "Don't sweat the others, they'll come around."

He jogs forward to catch Daren.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch09a-Pinecones, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Pinecones)


ID: B2-Ch09a-Sleep2
TEXT:

I haul both packs out of the tent, pull the stakes, and roll the canvas. After handing the bundle back to Hadrik, I carry Melindra's pack down to the gravel bank. She sits on a flat boulder, scraping the last crumbs of porcupine pie from a tin plate.

"Here," I say, tossing the pack beside her. "You left this."

"Right. Thanks." She catches the strap.

"I have to admit, I was surprised when you told Kate the truth this morning," I say. "I expected you to cling to my arm and play the scandalous couple again."

Melindra stares at me, genuine offense flickering across her face.

"What kind of monster do you think I am?" she asks. "You gave me a place to sleep. Why would I throw you under the wagon for it?"

I blink. "So you actually can give a damn about someone else."

"What is that supposed to mean?"

"Yesterday, you went out of your way to poke every hornets' nest in camp," I say. "You skip names, say whatever crosses your mind, and treat everyone's irritation like free entertainment."

"Why should I care if they're irritated?" Melindra crosses her arms. "I don't need them as friends. I need to reach Ollendor."

"Do you need them as enemies?"

"Eiden never held it against me."

"Most people aren't Eiden."

"That doesn't mean I have to rewrite myself to suit strangers," she snaps.

"Nobody's asking for a new personality," I say. "Just stop actively provoking the people walking beside you. Unless you'd rather spend every night on this road unable to ask a companion for a spare canvas?"

Her brow furrows, mouth tightening into a stubborn pout.

"Fine," she mutters. "I'll dial it back. A little. Only a little."

"Progress is progress," I say. "We should move. Everyone else has their packs on."

Melindra hoists hers, and we join the others. She takes the lead along the riverbank, and we head south into the woods.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch09a-Pinecones, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Pinecones)


ID: B2-Ch09a-Kick
TEXT:

"Doesn't sound like any standard enchantment!" Daren bellows in my ear. "I could attempt a dispel, but without knowing the structure, I risk making it permanent. Curses are my field; this is pure air manipulation. Melindra has to undo it herself."

"She said to enjoy my morning," I yell back. "She won't drop it on request."

"Then I'll talk to her after we pack," Daren shouts. "Go eat."

Flower trots over, her mouth shaping syllables lost in the howling gale inside my head.

"--Barry--hungry--make something--?"

"WHAT?" I shout.

She steps in and screams into my face. "Do you want breakfast?"

"Rations in my pack, but I'll never refuse your cooking! What do you have?"

"Trampler stew or archer porcupine pie!" she yells back. "Arena recipes! Two seconds!"

Plates of steaming meat and pastry materialize in my hands. I wolf down the food, pack my tent, and return the poles to Hadrik, the roar in my ears never dropping in volume.

The camp finishes packing. Overhead, the sky darkens from slate to bruised iron. A line of glowing blue text drifts across my vision.

"Daren has been asking if you're ready for ten seconds," Leila writes.

"What?" I blurt, turning around.

The whole group is staring at me. Daren's arms are crossed, eyebrows raised.

"YES! READY TO GO!" I shout at the top of my lungs.

Daren covers his face with one armored gauntlet, then turns on Melindra. He points at me, points at the trail, and gestures forcefully with both hands. She rolls her eyes, traces a lazy circle in the air with two fingers, and snaps her wrist.

The roar vanishes. Normal forest birdsong floods back into my ears.

"Spell's gone," Melindra calls out. "He can hear."

"Finally," Daren sighs. "Melindra, lead the way. We've burned enough daylight."

She takes the front, and we set off south along the river.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch09a-Pinecones, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Pinecones)


ID: B2-Ch09a-Pinecones
TEXT:

For the next hour, we follow the river's curve through the pines. Every few hundred paces, we pass lone mages on their knees, frantically stuffing pinecones into burlap sacks without so much as glancing in our direction.

"What are they doing?" Hadrik scratches his beard, watching two robed figures furiously raking the needles. "Aren't they supposed to test if the cones glow in their hands? Why bag hundreds of regular ones?"

choice(""Maybe they found a detector, and they're taking the pinecones to it."", B2-Ch09a-Detector, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Detector)
choice(""Maybe they're planning to take them to one of the sages."", B2-Ch09a-Sages, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Sages)
choice(""Maybe they're trying to go for quantity over quality."", B2-Ch09a-Quality, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Quality)


ID: B2-Ch09a-Detector
TEXT:

"Maybe they found a detector, and they're taking the pinecones to it."

"Doubtful," Melindra says. "A detector fits in one hand. You'd carry it into the woods."

"Unless someone else owns it and charges a fee for testing," I say.

"Perhaps," Melindra allows.

"Regardless," Daren says, scanning the trees, "they've worked out that raw mana won't ignite them."

"They still don't know the cones only grow in sacred groves," Melindra adds. "They can fill ten wagons out here and find nothing."

"The contestants aren't what concerns me," Daren says, looking up through the branches. "The sky has been turning black since dawn. A storm is coming."

"Do you get bad storms here?" Hadrik asks Flower.

"Rarely," Flower says. "When they hit in summer, they tear up the whole forest."

"Watch for falling timber," Daren warns. "And expect a soaking. We'll need a fire once it passes."

"I can raise an ice dome," Kate offers.

"And I can spin heat orbs around us!" Flower adds. "No need to build a fire."

"Conserve your reserves," Daren says. "Tyrath's hunting parties are still out there."

"Tyrath doesn't have an infinite supply of elite beasts," Melindra says. "He can't afford to throw his best troops at one group twice in a row. By the time we run into-- Whoa."

She stops dead, leaning back as if she nearly walked into a plate of glass. Her eyes lock onto an empty patch of forest air.

"See something?" Hadrik reaches out toward the empty space.

"Don't touch it!" Melindra snaps, catching his wrist. "I don't know what it does yet."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch09a-Buddies, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Buddies)


ID: B2-Ch09a-Sages
TEXT:

"Maybe they're planning to take them to one of the sages."

"Why would a sage help them?" Hadrik asks. "We're competing against them."

"A bounty," Kate says. "Bring a sage the right cone, buy a spot in his retinue for the next round."

"Regardless," Daren says, scanning the trees, "they've worked out that raw mana won't ignite them."

"They still don't know the cones only grow in sacred groves," Melindra adds. "They can fill ten wagons out here and find nothing."

"The contestants aren't what concerns me," Daren says, looking up through the branches. "The sky has been turning black since dawn. A storm is coming."

"Do you get bad storms here?" Hadrik asks Flower.

"Rarely," Flower says. "When they hit in summer, they tear up the whole forest."

"Watch for falling timber," Daren warns. "And expect a soaking. We'll need a fire once it passes."

"I can raise an ice dome," Kate offers.

"And I can spin heat orbs around us!" Flower adds. "No need to build a fire."

"Conserve your reserves," Daren says. "Tyrath's hunting parties are still out there."

"Tyrath doesn't have an infinite supply of elite beasts," Melindra says. "He can't afford to throw his best troops at one group twice in a row. By the time we run into-- Whoa."

She stops dead, leaning back as if she nearly walked into a plate of glass. Her eyes lock onto an empty patch of forest air.

"See something?" Hadrik reaches out toward the empty space.

"Don't touch it!" Melindra snaps, catching his wrist. "I don't know what it does yet."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch09a-Buddies, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Buddies)


ID: B2-Ch09a-Quality
TEXT:

"Maybe they're trying to go for quantity over quality."

"Dump a thousand cones at the finish line and hope one counts?" Hadrik grins. "Nobody's that thick."

"Rules didn't forbid bringing mundane pinecones," I say. "If you can't test them, haul them all."

"Regardless," Daren says, scanning the trees, "they've worked out that raw mana won't ignite them."

"They still don't know the cones only grow in sacred groves," Melindra adds. "They can fill ten wagons out here and find nothing."

"The contestants aren't what concerns me," Daren says, looking up through the branches. "The sky has been turning black since dawn. A storm is coming."

"Do you get bad storms here?" Hadrik asks Flower.

"Rarely," Flower says. "When they hit in summer, they tear up the whole forest."

"Watch for falling timber," Daren warns. "And expect a soaking. We'll need a fire once it passes."

"I can raise an ice dome," Kate offers.

"And I can spin heat orbs around us!" Flower adds. "No need to build a fire."

"Conserve your reserves," Daren says. "Tyrath's hunting parties are still out there."

"Tyrath doesn't have an infinite supply of elite beasts," Melindra says. "He can't afford to throw his best troops at one group twice in a row. By the time we run into-- Whoa."

She stops dead, leaning back as if she nearly walked into a plate of glass. Her eyes lock onto an empty patch of forest air.

"See something?" Hadrik reaches out toward the empty space.

"Don't touch it!" Melindra snaps, catching his wrist. "I don't know what it does yet."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch09a-Buddies, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Buddies)


ID: B2-Ch09a-Buddies
TEXT:

"A spell?" Daren steps forward, hand on his hilt. "I don't sense any aura."

"It's Eiden's work," Melindra says. "Layered to evade casual detection. The signature is his, but the construct is unfamiliar. Custom architecture."

"I've tripped over these across half the continent this past week," Arraka chimes in from the amulet. "He's been seeding them everywhere since his return. Weaving something that dense takes hours of focus."

"What does it do?" Kate asks, keeping her distance.

"Do I look like an academy scholar?" Arraka snorts. "You don't decipher five-layer primordial weaves with a glance. But it's dormant. Primed for a trigger."

"A trigger for what?" Hadrik asks. "The calamity he threatened? Or anti-lessathi wards? Could be landmines keyed to lessathi blood."

Leila steps back immediately, putting a thick pine trunk between herself and the clearing.

"Think about it," Hadrik continues. "Eiden hates them. He gave Barry that threat for a reason, and he slaughtered them in the Still Winter. Wouldn't take much to finish the job."

"What are you talking about?" Melindra mutters, still tracing the invisible boundary with her fingertips. "Eiden didn't slaughter lessathi in the Still Winter. He sided with them."

Hadrik stops. "He did what?"

Melindra freezes, her hand pulling back. "I shouldn't have said that."

"Eiden fought against stillwaters?" Daren's voice sharpens.

"Oh, he stacked them like cordwood," Arraka laughs. "Almost as many as I did!"

"You?" Kate looks down at the amulet. "Weren't you leading the stillwaters?"

"Therius led them," Arraka says with immense satisfaction. "I was merely sharing his frame. We weren't on speaking terms--wrestling for control every waking minute. But the moment he fell asleep, the body was mine. I tore through his camp before sunrise. Half the army dead before breakfast! When Therius woke up to a carpet of corpses, none of the survivors ever looked at him the same way. He had to govern by terror until the war ended. Gods, those were glorious times."

"Why did the war start?" Kate demands. "Why were the stillwaters and lessathi fighting? Why would Eiden back the lessathi?"

"Do you think I took notes?" Arraka cackles. "I just told you I gutted half my own officers for fun. Politics bored me then, and they bore me now."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch09a-Puzzle, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Puzzle)


ID: B2-Ch09a-Puzzle
TEXT:

"I take it you aren't filling in the gaps," Daren says, looking at Melindra.

"I've said too much already," Melindra replies, eyes forward. "I gave Eiden my word centuries ago not to discuss Still Winter specifics. I keep my promises."

"Of course," Daren mutters.

"Whatever this construct is, steer around it," Melindra says. "No point gambling on a hair-trigger. Move."

We veer wide around the clearing and cut deeper into the timber. Over the next few hours, the breeze sharpens into gusting wind, rattling the pines while the overcast thickens to slate. Hadrik and Daren trade old war stories, but Melindra and Flower walk side by side, murmuring in an unbroken exchange of their own.

#if(v_b2_ch8_energizer == 0) {
They find common ground with alarming speed--rocks, ruins, rare spells--and the chatter never pauses.

Past midday, we call a halt for lunch. Flower needs to conserve mana, so we eat cold rations. I take a mossy stump, unbuckle my pack, and crack open a sleeve of biscuits from home.

"Barry, is that all you're eating?" Flower asks, trotting over. "I can conjure a proper meal, it takes no time."

"Rations are fine, I'm not starving," I say. "Besides, I missed these. Bakery down the lane from my cabin in the Western Continent. Nowhere else gets the crust right."

Melindra glances up from behind Flower, eyes narrowing with sudden interest.

}
#if(v_b2_ch8_energizer != 0) {
They find common ground with alarming speed--rocks, ruins, rare spells--and the chatter never pauses.

Past midday, we call a halt for lunch. Flower needs to conserve mana, so we eat cold rations. I take a mossy stump, unbuckle my pack, and crack open a sleeve of biscuits from home.

"Barry, is that all you're eating?" Flower asks, trotting over. "I can conjure a proper meal, it takes no time."

"Rations are fine, I'm not starving," I say. "Besides, I missed these. Bakery down the lane from my cabin in the Western Continent. Nowhere else gets the crust right."

}
#if(v_b2_ch1_puzzle2 == 1 && v_ch8_first_puzzle == 1) {
"Oh," Flower says. "There was something else."

"Go ahead," I say, biting into a biscuit.

"Remember that puzzle piece from the castle in Thilias?"

"I remember."

"Melindra knows where another one is!" Flower's eyes light up. "She found one centuries ago, tossed it because she didn't care to hunt the rest, but she remembers the spot--an abandoned cellar in Ollendor's lower quarter."

"Convenient," I say.

"Daren mentioned you found a piece back in the underground ruins where we met," Flower adds, clasping her hands together. "Do you mind handing it over, or are you collecting them too?"

}
#if(v_b2_ch1_puzzle2 == 1 && v_ch8_first_puzzle != 1) {
"Oh," Flower says. "There was something else."

"Go ahead," I say, biting into a biscuit.

"Remember that puzzle piece from the castle in Thilias?"

"I remember."

"Melindra knows where another one is!" Flower's eyes light up. "She found one centuries ago, tossed it because she didn't care to hunt the rest, but she remembers the spot--an abandoned cellar in Ollendor's lower quarter."

"Convenient," I say.

"Have you run across any other pieces since reaching the island?" Flower asks.

"Just the one in Thilias."

"Awww," Flower pouts. "Worth asking. I'll check with Hadrik and Daren."

}
#if(v_b2_ch1_puzzle2 == 0 && v_ch8_first_puzzle == 1) {
"Oh," Flower says. "There was something else."

"Go ahead," I say, biting into a biscuit.

"Remember the hidden corridor in Thilias Castle?"

"Vaguely, yeah."

"Inside was a carved puzzle piece left by an ancient Varathian architect," Flower says eagerly. "And an inscription! It said assembling the whole set unlocks the architect's greatest secret."

"Sounds like quite the prize."

"Daren told me you found a piece in the ruins where we met," Flower says, leaning in with wide eyes. "Could I have it, or are you solving the puzzle yourself?"

}
#if(v_b2_ch1_puzzle2 == 0 && v_ch8_first_puzzle != 1) {
"Oh," Flower says. "There was something else."

"Go ahead," I say, biting into a biscuit.

"Remember the hidden corridor in Thilias Castle?"

"Vaguely, yeah."

"Inside was a carved puzzle piece left by an ancient Varathian architect," Flower says eagerly. "And an inscription! It said assembling the whole set unlocks the architect's greatest secret."

"Sounds like quite the prize."

"Have you stumbled across any other pieces while travelling?" Flower asks.

"Can't say I have."

"Awww," Flower pouts. "Worth asking. I'll check with Hadrik and Daren."

}
choice(""I'd rather hold onto it, if you don't mind."", B2-Ch09a-Coaxed, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Coaxed, v_b2_ch9_puzzle_give = 0) if (v_ch8_first_puzzle == 1)
choice(""You can have it, if you want."", B2-Ch09a-Coaxed, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Coaxed, v_b2_ch9_puzzle_give = 1) if (v_ch8_first_puzzle == 1)
choice(""Why do you care about these puzzle pieces so much?"", B2-Ch09a-Coaxed, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Coaxed, v_b2_ch9_puzzle_give = 2) if (v_ch8_first_puzzle != 1)
choice(""Sounds like a good idea. Good luck."", B2-Ch09a-Coaxed, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Coaxed, v_b2_ch9_puzzle_give = 3) if (v_ch8_first_puzzle != 1)


ID: B2-Ch09a-Coaxed
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch9_puzzle_give == 0) {
 "I'd rather hold onto it, if you don't mind."

"Awww," Flower says. "Guess I'll have to search those ruins again someday."

She gives me a bright nod. "Enjoy your biscuits, Barry!"

}
#if(v_b2_ch9_puzzle_give == 1) {
 "You can have it, if you want."

"Really?" Her face lights up.

"I wasn't doing anything with it," I say.

I dig into the bottom pouch of my pack, retrieve the carved stone relic, and place it in her hands. Flower turns it over in her palms, beaming.

"Thank you, Barry! I'm going to find every single one!"

"Go for it," I say.

"Enjoy your biscuits!"

}
#if(v_b2_ch9_puzzle_give == 2) {
 "Why do you care about these puzzle pieces so much?"

"Because it's a treasure hunt!" Flower says, bouncing on her heels. "And I want to know what the architect hid at the end."

"Hard to argue with that," I say.

"I'll go ask the others," she chirps. "Enjoy your biscuits!"

}
#if(v_b2_ch9_puzzle_give == 3) {
 "Sounds like a good idea. Good luck."

"Thanks!" Flower chirps. "Enjoy your biscuits!"

}
I give a two-finger salute with my biscuit hand as Flower heads off. I finish the snack, dust the crumbs off my trousers, and stow the wrapper.

Across the clearing, Kate sits by herself against the roots of a pine, chewing dried fruit. Nearby, Hadrik and Daren have dragged Leila into refereeing some convoluted rock-tossing contest, the rules of which seem to change every three throws. Flower and Melindra have already resumed their low murmur by the trail.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch09a-Kate-path, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Kate-path) if (v_b2_ch8_energizer == 3)
choice("Continue", B2-Ch09a-Energizer-path, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Energizer-path) if (v_b2_ch8_energizer == 1)
choice("Continue", B2-Ch09a-Feelings, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Feelings) if (v_b2_ch8_energizer == 0 || v_b2_ch8_energizer == 2)


ID: B2-Ch09a-Kate-path
TEXT:

With time to spare, I pull my notebook from my pack and flip through spell diagrams.

When the group shoulders gear, I tuck the book away and strap my pack tight.

"Brace yourselves," Daren calls out. "The wind is picking up fast. Stick close, watch the canopy, and keep clear of leaning trunks."

"This way," Melindra says, setting off. "Follow me."

We follow her for half an hour as the wind rises to a gale. Lightning splits the canopy, and the sky opens in a torrential deluge. Within seconds, water pours off our shoulders in sheets, driven sideways by gusts that sting like thrown gravel. The treetops groan and thrash like breaking masts.

"Tighten up!" Daren shouts over the howling wind. "Watch for lightning strikes! Stay under the tallest cover and do not take to the air! Keep moving!"

A crack echoes through the timber as an uprooted pine crashes down fifty paces ahead, sending moss and splintered branches flying. We skirt the debris and press on.

For twenty grueling minutes, we march into the teeth of the gale. Thunder detonates overhead with the force of siege spells, rattling teeth in jaws. The wind pushes hard enough to halt a step mid-stride, and the rain turns the forest floor into a churning torrent of mud.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch09a-Drying, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Drying)


ID: B2-Ch09a-Energizer-path
TEXT:

Kate is sitting under the pine roots, radiating pure frost. I leave my pack by the stump and walk over.

She gives me a sideways glance, then goes back to chewing dry cheese without acknowledging my presence.

"Kate," I say, stopping in front of her. "Can we talk?"

"Why?" Her tone could freeze boiling oil. "What is there to discuss?"

"You've been furious since morning."

"Have I? I hadn't noticed." Another bite of cheese, eyes firmly on the pine needles.

"You are, and you know it. I didn't mean to upset you."

#if(v_ch11_romance_rose == 1 && v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
"Maybe consider that before bedding the enemy," she says.

"Melindra isn't our enemy."

"She's a stillwater," Kate snaps, eyes finally flashing up to meet mine. "Eiden's friend. She hoards information and sits on her hands while we bleed in battle. What were you thinking?"

"I know you don't trust her, but--"

"I'm done," Kate says, rising to her feet.

"Where are you going?"

"Somewhere quiet to finish eating. Do not follow me."

She takes three strides, then halts and looks back over her shoulder.

"And next time you promise Rose you'll return for her, try taking your own word seriously."

She disappears into the thicket without another word.

}
#if(v_ch11_romance_rose == 0 || v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
"Maybe consider that before bedding the enemy," she says.

"Melindra isn't our enemy."

"She's a stillwater," Kate snaps, eyes finally flashing up to meet mine. "Eiden's friend. She hoards information and sits on her hands while we bleed in battle. What were you thinking?"

"I know you don't trust her, but--"

"I'm done," Kate says, rising to her feet.

"Where are you going?"

"Somewhere quiet to finish eating. Do not follow me."

She disappears into the thicket without another word.

}
I walk back to the stump, haul out my notebook, and bury myself in spell diagrams until Daren calls for departure.

"Brace yourselves," Daren warns, strapping his buckler tight. "The gale is picking up. Stay close, watch for falling timber."

"This way," Melindra calls out from the trail. "Follow me."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch09a-Energizer-path2, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Energizer-path2)


ID: B2-Ch09a-Energizer-path2
TEXT:

Ten minutes down the trail, my skull slams into a wall of agony. A spike drives straight behind my eyes, knees buckling as cold sweat breaks across my neck. Violent nausea rises in my throat, forcing me to double over against a pine trunk.

"Barry?" Daren halts, turning back. "What's happened?"

"Headache," I choke out, eyes squeezed shut against the throbbing light.

"The energizer wore off!" Melindra chirps. "Mine's next, probably."

She mutters a quick cadence, weaves her fingers, and a faint gust settles over her shoulders as she refreshes her own spell.

"Energizer?" Daren's brow furrows, then clears in realization. "From last night. Let me guess--first time he's ever had one cast on him, and the backlash is hitting full force?"

"Exactly," Melindra says. "Easily fixed with a second cast."

#if(v_b2_ch9_cast1 == 0) {
"No," I wheeze, gripping my temples. "I told you no second cast. Daren, can't you heal the headache and nausea?"

"I can," Daren says grimly, "but I won't. Not unless your life is in danger. Channeling white magic into a heart strained by energizer backlash risks cardiac arrest. You're far safer letting her recast the energizer. It taxes the heart, but gradually. One re-cast won't kill you."

}
#if(v_b2_ch9_cast1 == 1) {
"Wait," I manage through gritted teeth. "Daren, can't you just heal the headache?"

"I can," Daren says grimly, "but I won't. Not unless your life is in danger. Channeling white magic into a heart strained by energizer backlash risks cardiac arrest. You're far safer letting her recast the energizer. It taxes the heart, but gradually. One re-cast won't kill you."

}
choice("Let Melindra cast the spell", B2-Ch09a-Energizer-path3, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Energizer-path3, v_b2_ch9_cast2 = 1)
choice("Don't let Melindra cast the spell", B2-Ch09a-Energizer-stat, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Energizer-stat, v_b2_ch9_cast2 = 0)


ID: B2-Ch09a-Energizer-path3
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch9_cast2 == 1) {
 "Fine," I groan. "Melindra... cast it."

She steps in and chants a sharp string of syllables, palms hovering over my chest. A warm breeze washes through my ribcage. The iron band around my skull dissolves, and the nausea drains away, though my limbs feel hollow rather than truly rested.

"Feels weaker this time," I say, rolling my shoulders.

"Diminishing returns," Melindra says. "Second casts are always shorter and lighter. It will carry you to camp."

"Then let's make time," Daren says. "Keep moving."

We follow her for half an hour as the wind rises to a gale. Lightning splits the canopy, and the sky opens in a torrential deluge. Within seconds, water pours off our shoulders in sheets, driven sideways by gusts that sting like thrown gravel. The treetops groan and thrash like breaking masts.

"Tighten up!" Daren shouts over the howling wind. "Watch for lightning strikes! Stay under the tallest cover and do not take to the air! Keep moving!"

A crack echoes through the timber as an uprooted pine crashes down fifty paces ahead, sending moss and splintered branches flying. We skirt the debris and press on.

For twenty grueling minutes, we march into the teeth of the gale. Thunder detonates overhead with the force of siege spells, rattling teeth in jaws. The wind pushes hard enough to halt a step mid-stride, and the rain turns the forest floor into a churning torrent of mud.

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch09a-Drying, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Drying)


ID: B2-Ch09a-Energizer-stat
TEXT:

set(v_is_dead,1) if (v_reflexes < 4)
set(v_is_dead,0) if (v_reflexes == 4)
#if(v_reflexes < 4) {
"I'll deal with the headache," I say. "Postponing the crash only makes the debt worse."

"That's your choice," Daren says, "but don't let it dull your guard."

"I have it under control," I lie, pressing my palm hard against my throbbing temple.

"Move out," Daren calls.

We follow Melindra into the teeth of the rising wind. Lightning cracks across the canopy, and rain falls like a collapsing ceiling, blinding and relentless. Branches whip sideways in the gale.

"Stay tight!" Daren bellows through the roar. "Watch the crown branches! Keep moving!"

My skull pounds in sync with every footstep, bile rising in my throat. The storm is a blur of gray needles and howling wind.

"Look out!" Daren roars.

A massive trunk rips free of the loam, crashing into the brush ten paces ahead. The ground shudders under the impact. I barely register the shockwave before Daren's voice screams again.

"Barry, behind you!"

My sluggish limbs refuse to fire. A crushing weight drops from above, snapping bone and spine into the wet earth. Darkness swallows the gale.

}
#if(v_reflexes == 4) {
"I'll deal with the headache," I say. "Postponing the crash only makes the debt worse."

"That's your choice," Daren says, "but don't let it dull your guard."

"I have it under control," I lie, pressing my palm hard against my throbbing temple.

"Move out," Daren calls.

We follow Melindra into the teeth of the rising wind. Lightning cracks across the canopy, and rain falls like a collapsing ceiling, blinding and relentless. Branches whip sideways in the gale.

"Stay tight!" Daren bellows through the roar. "Watch the crown branches! Keep moving!"

My skull pounds in sync with every footstep, bile rising in my throat. I force my eyes open against the deluge.

"Look out!" Daren roars.

A massive trunk rips free of the loam, crashing into the brush ten paces ahead. The shockwave rattles up my shins. Before the echo dies, Daren's voice screams from behind.

"Barry, behind you!"

No time to look. Reflexes honed by the device fire on instinct--I dive sideways into the mud as a five-ton oak smashes into the spot where my boots were planted a fraction of a second ago. The blast of displaced air throws wet mulch across my back.

I scramble up against the fallen bark, heart hammering against my ribs. A heartbeat slower, and I would be paste.

}
