ID: B2-Ch09a-Furiously
TEXT:

"That's enough!" Daren storms over through the downpour, fury in his eyes. "You're half-blind from that headache and you nearly got squashed flat! You are not walking this forest impaired. Melindra, cast the damn spell on him right now. No more debating!"

"Agreed," I say, wiping mud from my cheek. "Way too close. Melindra, go ahead."

She steps up through the rain, chanting sharply, and presses her fingers against my chest. The pounding behind my temples melts into a faint hum, and the nausea drains away, though my body feels hollowed rather than restored.

"Weaker this time," I say, getting to my feet.

"Diminishing returns," Melindra says. "Second casts are always shorter and lighter. It will carry you to camp."

"Good," Daren shouts over the thunder. "Keep moving and watch the treetops!"

For twenty grueling minutes, we march into the teeth of the gale. Thunder detonates overhead with the force of siege spells, rattling teeth in jaws. The wind pushes hard enough to halt a step mid-stride, and the rain turns the forest floor into a churning torrent of mud.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch09a-Drying, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Drying)


ID: B2-Ch09a-Feelings
TEXT:

Across the clearing, Kate is sitting against the pine roots, eating alone.
Melindra's claim from yesterday drifts back into my head. "She has feelings for you."
I shouldn't touch it. Asking outright is asking for frostbite. But letting it sit unanswered guarantees it will rattle around in my skull for the rest of the week.
A subtle, indirect approach. Work it into the conversation naturally, casual, no pressure.

I leave my pack by the stump and walk over.

She sets her tin plate in the grass and looks up, giving me her full attention. "What is it?"

Right. Subtle. Casual.

"There's something on my mind from yesterday," I say.

"Go on."

"You don't happen to have feelings for me, do you?"

Tactless. Direct hit. Absolutely zero subtlety.

Kate stares at me like I just grew a third ear. "...Have you lost your mind?"

"Wait, don't conjure an icicle!" I hold up both hands. "It wasn't my idea. Melindra brought it up."

Her jaw tightens. "Melindra. What did she say?"

"She claimed that look you gave her yesterday--when she played the clingy partner--meant you were jealous."

"Oh." Kate's gaze drifts to the dark grass. Her expression closes off, guarded, calculating. "I see."

She lifts her head, locking eyes with me again.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch09a-Feelings2, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Feelings2)


ID: B2-Ch09a-Feelings2
TEXT:

#if(v_ch11_romance_rose == 1 && v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
 "I was shocked when I saw it, yes," Kate says. "You'd been talking to her privately minutes before, and I wondered if you'd forgotten the promise you made Rose in Thilias. You told Rose you'd come back for her when the tournament ends."

"I remember," I say. "I promised."

"Keep it," Kate says. "She teases, but she took you at your word. She'll be waiting."

"I know. I'm not breaking it."

"I know Melindra was playing a game yesterday," Kate continues, "but stay on your guard with her. She isn't predictable. And as for me... you don't need to worry about any feelings. I'm happy for you and Rose. Just make sure you survive long enough to keep your word."

"Don't you mean survive long enough to win?" I smirk.

"Sure." A faint smile tugs at the corner of her lips. "Win."

She weaves frost between her fingers, shaping an ice dagger, and tosses it lightly from palm to palm.

"You're lucky I'm not in the running," she says. "With no protection magic and that crossbow, you'd never touch me while I'm hovering overhead in an ice cage raining icicles on your head."

}
#if(v_ch11_romance_rose == 0 || v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
 "I was startled when I saw the two of you, yes," Kate says. "You'd been alone with her just before, and I worried you were about to do something reckless. I know it was a joke now, but you still need to watch yourself around Melindra. Her motives are opaque. As for her claims... I look out for you because you're a friend, Barry. I don't want you making mistakes that get you killed."

"Hold on. 'Main' reason?" I raise an eyebrow. "Are there secondary reasons on file?"

She glares at me. "You are infuriating."

"You chose the phrasing, not me."

"Regardless of how you twist it," Kate says, "our focus belongs on the tournament and the objectives."

"Naturally."

"If you want to cross-examine my phrasing, wait until we survive this island."

"Don't you mean after I win?"

A faint smirk breaks through her scowl. "Right. After you win."

She weaves frost between her fingers, shaping an ice dagger, and tosses it lightly from palm to palm.

"You're lucky I'm not in the running," she says. "With no protection magic and that crossbow, you'd never touch me while I'm hovering overhead in an ice cage raining icicles on your head."

}
choice(""I don't know. I wouldn't be so sure about that."", B2-Ch09a-Feelings3, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Feelings3, v_b2_ch9_defeat = 1)
choice(""Yeah, I probably couldn't last ten seconds against you in an actual fight."", B2-Ch09a-Feelings3, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Feelings3, v_b2_ch9_defeat = 0)


ID: B2-Ch09a-Feelings3
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch9_defeat == 1) {
 "I don't know. I wouldn't be so sure about that."

"No?"

"Depends on the terrain," I say. "Underground or inside a fortress, flight is off the table. In the open, starting distance decides it. You take a beat to raise barriers against sudden rushes. If I close the gap before your casting settles, I have an opening. Wait--this is purely hypothetical, right? You wouldn't actually try to kill me if the brackets paired us up?"

"No," Kate says. "Would you?"

"I couldn't. No."

"Then we're both fortunate," she says, letting the ice dagger dissolve into vapor.

}
#if(v_b2_ch9_defeat == 0) {
 "Yeah, I probably couldn't last ten seconds against you in an actual fight."

"Admitting defeat that easily?" Kate arches an eyebrow. "From a man planning to win the Magium?"

"We aren't fighting each other," I say. "No reason to pretend I wouldn't end up skewered. Though--speaking of raining icicles--that was hypothetical, right? You wouldn't actually try to kill me if the tournament pitted us together?"

"Of course not."

She watches me for a quiet second.

"Would you?" she asks.

"I couldn't. No."

"Then we're both fortunate," she says, letting the ice dagger dissolve into vapor.

}
"Daren won't be that simple," Kate adds quietly. "You two are the only ones here chasing the prize. If you both reach the end, only one person walks away with the Magium. You'll have to face each other."

"I know."

We let the silence hang between us.

"I'll let you finish lunch," I say, stepping back. "I'm going to look through my notes on Eiden."

"Good luck," Kate says, picking up her plate.

Having finished my discussion with Kate, I head back towards my tree stump, in order to get some reading done before we leave.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch09a-Vicinity, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Vicinity) if (v_b2_ch8_energizer == 2)
