ID: B2-Ch09a-Drying
TEXT:

"Enough," Kate snaps.

Frost erupts from her boots, arching overhead in thick, crystalline ribs that snap together into a solid ice dome. The deafening roar of the gale drops to a muffled thud against the frozen shell.

"Kate," Daren says, wiping water from his eyes. "What are you doing?"

"Stopping," Kate says, wringing out her braid. "We're burning stamina for nothing. We're barely making half a mile an hour against this gale, and half of us will collapse before we reach an enemy."

"I agree with the ice mage," Melindra says, shaking water from her sleeves. "Marching into this is pointless. Rest and move when the squall blows out."

"If everyone agrees..." Daren looks around the frozen circle.

"Count me in," Hadrik grunts, squeezing a gallon of water from his beard. "I've swallowed enough rain for one lifetime."

"I'll dry everyone!" Flower cheers.

Sparks ignite around her palms, splitting into dozens of floating cherry-sized fire orbs. They dart across the dome, orbiting our soaked clothes in tight, humming spirals that hiss as clouds of steam billow into the air.

"Watch the beard, little one," Hadrik mutters, leaning back from a passing spark.

"I've done this hundreds of times," Flower laughs. "Nobody's getting singed!"

"I should hope not," Daren says warily, watching an orb glide past his cuirass.

Within a minute, our wool and leather are bone-dry and warm. Flower snaps her fingers, and the fire orbs wink out.

"Right!" Hadrik claps his hands, the sound cracking inside the ice shell. "We're trapped here until the sky clears. Let's pass the time before we go mad from boredom."

"With what?" Daren asks.

"Barry's notebook," Hadrik says. "Any new loopholes against Eiden? That's what we all want to hear."

"Or," Kate says, her voice flat ice, "we could avoid laying out battle tactics in front of Eiden's friend."

"Come on," Hadrik waves a hand. "He brushed her off like an annoying gnat two days ago. She isn't running off to report to him."

"She still calls him a friend," Kate says coldly. "What stops her from handing him every plan the moment they cross paths?"

Melindra scowls. "Do you want another blood oath on Daren's sword? I'll swear it right now, if it stops you whispering like conspirators every time I take a breath."

choice(""There's no need for you to take another enchanter's oath, Melindra."", B2-Ch09a-Oath, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Oath, v_b2_ch9_oath = 0)
choice(""Actually, that wouldn't be such a bad idea."", B2-Ch09a-Oath, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Oath, v_b2_ch9_oath = 1)


ID: B2-Ch09a-Oath
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch9_oath == 0) {
 "There's no need for you to take another enchanter's oath, Melindra."

"No?" Melindra arches an eyebrow. "What should I do, plug my ears?"

"Nothing," I say. "Because I haven't uncovered anything new on Eiden anyway."

"Great," Hadrik sighs, sliding down the ice wall. "Back to square one."

"Not entirely," I say. "Depends on how you define a weakness. Remember when he teleported everyone to that clearing after you went giant on day two?"

"Second day of the tournament, yeah," Hadrik says.

"He gathered every mage within two miles, including Daren, Kate, and me. But he didn't spot us until minutes later. His magical senses cover vast territory, but his conscious attention is finite. He can't track hundreds of individuals at once. He can be distracted."

"That isn't much of an exploit," Hadrik says.

"It's an edge," I reply. "Against someone with that much power, every blind spot matters."

"Fair enough," Hadrik says. "What about his past, then? Motivations. Why is he seeding those invisible spells across the continent?"

}
#if(v_b2_ch9_oath == 1) {
 "Actually, that wouldn't be such a bad idea."

"It certainly wouldn't hurt," Kate says.

Daren looks between us. "Are we really doing this?"

"Draw the steel, enchanter," Melindra says, stepping up to him and slicing the tip of her index finger along the edge of his blade.

A bead of crimson wells up. She holds her finger above the flat of the sword.

Daren sighs, angling the blade beneath the drop. "Swear the words and be done with it."

White light flares along the steel as the blood contacts the enchantment.

"I swear never to reveal your strategies to Eiden," Melindra recites flatly, "nor repeat any discussion that could be used to his tactical advantage."

She pauses, looking at Kate. "Satisfied, or should I add my shoe size?"

"It will do," Kate says.

Daren sweeps his gauntlet over the runes, sealing the vow, and the radiance fades.

"Thrilling," Hadrik says. "Now, Barry--what loopholes did you find?"

"None," I say. "Nothing since Daren's barrier interaction."

"Brilliant," Melindra smirks. "Rest easy knowing that profound secret is safe with me."

Hadrik groans. "Back to square one."

"Not entirely," I say. "Depends on how you define a weakness. Remember when he teleported everyone to that clearing after you went giant on day two?"

"Second day of the tournament, yeah," Hadrik says.

"He gathered every mage within two miles, including Daren, Kate, and me. But he didn't spot us until minutes later. His magical senses cover vast territory, but his conscious attention is finite. He can't track hundreds of individuals at once. He can be distracted."

"That isn't much of an exploit," Hadrik says.

"It's an edge," I reply. "Against someone with that much power, every blind spot matters."

"Fair enough," Hadrik says. "What about his past, then? Motivations. Why is he seeding those invisible spells across the continent?"

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch09a-Downhill, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09a-Downhill)


ID: B2-Ch09a-Downhill
TEXT:

"Good luck deciphering that," Melindra says. "I've known him six centuries and I have no idea what he's weaving."

"Trying to win back the golden fox, perhaps?" Arraka sneers from the amulet. "They had a lovers' quarrel after his performance in Olmnar. Not that they were on great terms before."

"The rot set in long before Olmnar," Melindra says, staring into the ice wall. "It started when his cities adopted slavery. The fox blamed Eiden for every atrocity that followed. She insisted that if he governed them with an iron fist, order would hold. But Eiden believed humans were inherently good--that if you gave them abundance and freedom, they would choose virtue on their own. We all saw how that ended."

"He just gave up?" Daren's voice cracks with outrage.

"Not immediately," Melindra says. "He spent centuries trying to guide them. Longer than your ancestors' bloodlines. He corrected them dozens of times. But every betrayal chipped away at him. The final break came with rulers he personally trusted--a king and queen whose names I forget. Charming rulers, beloved by their court. Then Eiden discovered their private arena, where political prisoners were torn apart by hounds for entertainment. He staged an exhibition of his own and fed the royal pair to their own dogs. Right after that, he told me he was done. That was when he closed his eyes."

"And left the rest to suffer?" Daren's fists clench. "What about the innocents under their boots?"

"He stopped differentiating," Melindra says flatly. "Called them sheep. Said they deserved whatever their greed produced. He even nudged the cruelty along when it amused him, claiming it was what the people chose. That was when Eleya broke with him completely, walling off her territory with extreme laws so his corruption wouldn't bleed into the woods. Eventually, Eiden vanished overseas. When he returned twenty years ago, found the poachers in Olmnar, and leveled the city... that was the formal end. He left Varathia again."

"And believed he solved the problem by incinerating the town," Arraka mocks. "When his own absence allowed the poachers to thrive. Complete fool."

"Wait," Hadrik scratches his head. "Weren't Eiden and the fox just Still Winter allies? Why make jokes about winning her heart? Why does their feud matter to us?"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch09b-Inseparable, v_current_scene = B2-Ch09b-Inseparable)
